Scenario: An HTML page has all its text in a default font, but the company logo requires the text to be in a specific color and size. Analysis: The HTML defines the text content, but CSS is required to change the presentation (color and size) to meet the design requirements. Code Example (CSS Rule): h1 { color: red; font-size: 24px; }

Scenario: A developer has a 100-page website and wants all headings to be blue. Analysis: Using an External Style Sheet is the most efficient method, as a single change in the .css file will update all 100 pages, following the Separation of Concerns principle. Code Example (HTML Link): <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">

Scenario: A developer wants to style all paragraphs differently from a specific paragraph with the unique ID "special-note". Analysis: They would use the Element Selector p for all paragraphs and the ID Selector #special-note for the unique one. Code Example (CSS): p { background-color: lightgrey; } #special-note { border: 2px solid red; }

Scenario: A button needs to have red text, a yellow background, and 10 pixels of space between the text and the border. Analysis: This requires the color, background-color, and padding properties. Code Example (CSS): button { color: red; background-color: yellow; padding: 10px; }
